      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mount Vernon was originally called Little Hunting Creek Plantation and spanned about 5,000 acres.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/topic/Mount-Vernon-historical-site-Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "George Washington renamed the plantation Mount Vernon in honor of Admiral Edward Vernon, under whom he served.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/topic/Mount-Vernon-historical-site-Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The estate, now a national historic landmark, is exempt from Virginia taxes as long as it is restored and maintained.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/topic/Mount-Vernon-historical-site-Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mount Vernon hosts a 4D Revolutionary War film and interactive exhibits that bring history to life.",
